% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 1
% composition-elements: duration (beat versus rhythm; quarter notes, paired eighths and quarter rests in 4/4), pitch (mi-so-la only), texture (unison song whose bars 1-2 repeat as a groove that one group can keep going as an ostinato under the rest), form (call and response in bars 3-4 and 7-8)
% composition-listen: Fela Kuti and Africa 70 — "Water No Get Enemy" (1975), from the album Expensive Shit
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kisTH3SFegc
% composition-bridge: Track one repeating part while other layers enter; hear that a groove can stay steady while the surface changes.
% composition-context: Fela Anikulapo Kuti (1938-1997) built Afrobeat in Lagos, Nigeria, from Yoruba music, highlife, jazz and funk, with long pieces in which drums, guitars, bass and horns lock together in layers and his words, in Nigerian Pidgin and Yoruba, criticise power. "Water No Get Enemy" (1975) takes its title from a proverb about water's strength. This three-note classroom song is a first lesson in keeping one part going, not an Afrobeat piece.
% composition-distance: Original melody, rhythm and words; no horn line, lyric, proverb wording, bass pattern or harmony from the recording is used. The words mention water only as a picture of something that keeps moving.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"4","role":"class","prompt":"What fills the last beat?","choices":["keep the rest","sing 'beat!' again","two quick claps"],"replyBars":1}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"5-8","role":"one group","prompt":"Which layer joins the groove?","choices":["clap the beat","tap the word rhythm","shaker ti-ti"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original Grade 1 groove song for call and response and for feeling beat against rhythm; bars 1-2 become an ostinato layer once the song is known
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4 with no pickup; pitch set mi-so-la only (E4, G4, A4), written range E4-A4; quarter notes, paired eighth notes and quarter rests only (no half notes); in one-bar units the form is groove (bars 1-2), call (bar 3), response (bar 4), groove again (bars 5-6), new call (bar 7), closing response (bar 8); bars 2, 4, 6 and 8 end with a quarter-rest breath; steps and minor thirds inside each phrase, and one perfect fourth (E4 up to A4) after the breath into each call
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 100 is the target; begin at 84. The written range E4-A4 sits well inside the young-unison C4-D5 range, and C major (mi-so-la on E-G-A) is the rehearsal key. Teach in steps: pat the beat while the teacher sings bars 1-2; class echoes bars 1-2 until secure; teacher sings the calls (bars 3 and 7), class answers (bars 4 and 8); sing the whole song; then one group repeats bars 1-2 as an ostinato all the way through while the rest sing the song, and the improv cues add a body-percussion layer
% dossier-accompaniment: %%style funk is the Band's generic funk groove, chosen for a steady layered pulse; it is not Afrobeat and must not be called Afrobeat or Nigerian. No chord symbols are written; the Band harmonises the three notes itself. Classroom layers: claps on the beat, pats on the word rhythm, shakers on steady eighths, and the bars 1-2 groove sung as an ostinato
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record, one syllable per note, every stressed syllable on a beat (DRIP, DROP, WA-ter ROLLS; WHO will KEEP the BEAT). Bars 3 and 7 are the leader's calls and bars 4 and 8 the class's answers. Sung throughout
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed grade, range and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on the melody, lyric or groove of "Water No Get Enemy"; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. Fela Kuti's composition and recording remain the copyright of their owners and are not included; the class listens through the linked official upload
% dossier-respect: Name Fela Anikulapo Kuti, Africa 70, Lagos and Nigeria, and the political history of his music (his Kalakuta compound, his arrests, his criticism of military government) at a level suited to Grade 1. Do not teach Afrobeat as a generic classroom drum loop, do not present this song or the Band's funk groove as Afrobeat, and keep "Afrobeat" (Fela's 1970s music) distinct from present-day "Afrobeats" pop
% dossier-review: Grade 1 classroom music teacher, Nigerian or Afrobeat-informed music educator, second score reader; all reviews still required
