N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: bar 1 G-E-G-A-G (quarter, quarter, two eighths, quarter), the groove motif; bar 2 A-G-E and a quarter rest; bar 3 the call A-A-G-G-A, ending high on la; bar 4 the answer G-G-E-E and a quarter rest, ending on mi; bars 5-6 repeat bars 1-2 exactly so the groove can be kept as an ostinato; bar 7 a new call A-G-A-A-G, ending on so; bar 8 the closing answer G-G-E-E and a rest, the same shape as bar 4. Every bar totals four quarter beats; the only pitches are E4, G4 and A4 (mi-so-la); range E4-A4; inside a phrase the largest leap is a minor third, and the only larger one is the perfect fourth E4-A4 after the rest into each call (bars 2-3 and 6-7); durations are quarters, eighths and quarter rests only. The song ends on mi, the resting tone of a mi-so-la song (there is no do at Grade 1). Originality: the groove (so-mi-so-la-so) and the endings (so-mi) are small mi-so-la playground patterns that are common property; the tune was checked against "Rain, Rain, Go Away", "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Lucy Locket" and the Fela recording's horn and vocal hooks and shares no phrase with them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 1 singers can keep bars 1-2 going as an ostinato against the calls, whether 100 is too fast for the answers, and whether "two quick claps" in bar 4 lands cleanly have not been tested. The Band's funk groove under three sung notes has not been heard by a teacher. A classroom trial, a Nigerian or Afrobeat-informed educator and a second score reader remain required.
